# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored and must stay that way.
# Demo mode (`bun run backend:demo`) needs none of this.
#
# OR RUN `make env`, which writes .env for you: it derives this worktree's port, database and public
# URLs from its slot, inherits the model key from an env file that already has one rather than
# asking you to copy it. It generates NOTHING: the admin token it used to mint is retired (#391b).
# Re-running rewrites only the derived keys and
# carries everything else across, so a value you set here by hand survives. See docs/WORKTREES.md.
#
# This file stays the inventory: every setting the backend reads is listed below, whichever way the
# .env got written.

# Postgres. The app never creates the database — `./dev db up` does. The user is `eait_app` and not
# the `eait` the container makes: that one is a SUPERUSER, and a superuser bypasses the row-level
# security `store.pg.ts` puts on every user-scoped table.
EAIT__BACKEND__DATABASE_URL=postgres://eait_app:eait@127.0.0.1:5433/eait
# The store's connection ceiling. It bounds concurrent store CALLS, not statements: since the
# row-level-security work every method runs inside a transaction for its whole body, so a call
# holds its connection until it commits. Default 25.
EAIT__BACKEND__DATABASE_MAX_CONNECTIONS=25

# OpenRouter. Every photo is a billed vision call on this key.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_API_KEY=
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_MODEL=x-ai/grok-4.5
# The coach — the model behind a QUESTION in chat, with the user's meals and health data behind
# tools. Text only, so it need not be the vision model above, and its own setting so the two can
# move independently. This account reaches x-ai and Chinese-vendor models through OpenRouter;
# deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro-0813 is the cheaper alternative if the bill matters more than the prose.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_CHAT_MODEL=x-ai/grok-4.6
# One sentence about the plate while the analyzer works, on a model that does NOT reason: 0.9 s to
# first token measured 2026-09-05 (grok-4.5 refuses to switch reasoning off). Empty disables it.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_GLANCE_MODEL=x-ai/grok-4.3
# low | medium | high, sent as reasoning.effort on every schema call. Empty = the model decides
# (grok-4.5: median 37 s to first token). Flip only after the n=30 bake-off in docs/ACCURACY.md.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=
# Where the chat-completions call goes. Point staging at a proxy, a gateway, or a fixture server
# without touching code.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions
# How long one model call may hang. There was none, and a provider that accepts the connection and
# never answers held the request, the photo and a worker slot until the process restarted.
# SENT TO THE APP as ProfileResponse.limits.modelCallTimeoutMs, which is how the phone sizes its own
# wait — so this is the number both sides reason from and there is no second copy to keep in step.
# Floored at MIN_MODEL_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS (10s): zero is a budget of nothing, not "no limit".
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=90000
# The completion bound sent with every call. Omitting it does not mean "no limit": the provider
# substitutes the model's own ceiling (65536) and reserves the whole of it against the balance
# before routing, so an unbounded request is refused as a 402 on a balance that would have covered
# the real call many times over. A measured meal analysis is 1614 completion tokens on grok-4.5,
# reasoning included, so this is ~10x headroom. Raise it if a call is ever logged as truncated: the
# reservation only has to clear the balance, it is not charged (16000 bills ~$0.01 and needs ~$0.10
# of balance to route). Deleting this line is safe — it falls back to the same value in
# `configDefaults()`. What must never happen is a bound reaching the provider unset.
EAIT__BACKEND__LLM_MAX_TOKENS=16000

# Loopback by default. Do not bind 0.0.0.0 without deciding to.
EAIT__BACKEND__HOST=127.0.0.1
EAIT__BACKEND__PORT=8484

# Spend. 0 disables a DAILY cap entirely — set EAIT__BACKEND__GLOBAL_DAILY_ANALYSIS_CAP if the API is public.
#
# There is no free tier. An account gets EAIT__BACKEND__FREE_ANALYSES analyses over its lifetime — the
# onboarding sample, photo or typed — and every later one is refused with 402 until
# the RevenueCat webhook below has written an entitlement. With the webhook unset nobody is ever
# entitled, so the server is a one-verdict demo of itself.
#
# EAIT__BACKEND__PAID_DAILY_PHOTO_CAP applies only to an entitled account. EAIT__BACKEND__GLOBAL_DAILY_ANALYSIS_CAP is
# above it: a subscription buys a larger share of the instance budget, never an exemption from it.
# DELIBERATELY NOT SET, like the deploy example. `bun test` loads this file's copy at `.env`, and
# the landing copy is written against whatever it says — so a pinned number here turned a fresh
# checkout's `bun run check` red on copy nobody had touched. Unset means the shipped default.
# EAIT__BACKEND__FREE_ANALYSES=1
EAIT__BACKEND__PAID_DAILY_PHOTO_CAP=200
EAIT__BACKEND__GLOBAL_DAILY_ANALYSIS_CAP=500

# How long a meal proposed in chat stays confirmable.
EAIT__BACKEND__PENDING_TTL_MINUTES=30

# Upload limits. These are SERVER-AUTHORITATIVE and are sent to the app in the profile response, so
# it offers exactly as many photo slots as this instance will accept. Do not also hardcode them in
# the client: two numbers that must agree are two numbers that will not.
EAIT__BACKEND__MAX_UPLOAD_MB=20
EAIT__BACKEND__MAX_PHOTOS_PER_MEAL=4

# ── Sign in with Apple / Google ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CLIENT IDS ARE NOT SECRETS — they identify the app, they do not authorise anything. They are here
# because the backend must know which audiences to ACCEPT: a token minted for a different app is a
# valid token and an invalid login, and only the audience check catches that.
# Empty list = that provider is off, and its route refuses rather than verifying without the check.
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_AUDIENCES=com.eait.fit.ios,com.eait.fit.ios.preview,com.eait.fit.ios.dev
EAIT__BACKEND__GOOGLE_AUDIENCES=

# ── Onboarding in a browser (docs/WEB_ONBOARDING.md) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The Google OAuth WEB client, which is a different client from the app's iOS one. BOTH OR NEITHER:
# with either empty, every path under /start answers 404 — the same shape as the admin.
#
# The id must ALSO be listed in EAIT__BACKEND__GOOGLE_AUDIENCES above — the server REFUSES TO START
# otherwise, because the token this flow gets back carries the id as its audience and every callback
# would fail verification after the user had already chosen their Google account. The SECRET is a
# real secret (redact() masks it, nothing prints it): Google requires one at
# the token endpoint for a web client (the app's native flow has none, which is why it carries none).
# Authorized redirect URI to register with Google:
#   ${EAIT__BACKEND__PUBLIC_API_URL}/start/auth/google/callback
EAIT__BACKEND__GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
EAIT__BACKEND__GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Sign in with Apple, in a browser. ALL FOUR OR NONE — with any of them empty the Apple button is
# simply not offered and its routes 404; /start still works with Google alone, and vice versa.
#
# IT IS A DIFFERENT CLIENT FROM THE APP'S. The app authorises as its BUNDLE ID; a browser authorises
# as a SERVICE ID. So the token that comes back here carries the Service ID as its audience, and the
# SERVICE ID must ALSO be listed in EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_AUDIENCES above, beside the bundle id — the
# server refuses to start otherwise, for the reason the Google id has the same rule. Keep the bundle
# id in that list: dropping it switches off sign-in in the app.
#
# Apple issues no client secret. It issues a KEY, and the secret is a five-minute ES256 JWT this
# server mints from it per request. The .p8 is the one real secret of the four; write it on ONE LINE
# with \n for the newlines (a .env file, a compose environment: block and an ansible template have
# three different answers to a literal newline and one answer to \n), BEGIN and END lines included.
# A value that is not a PKCS#8 PEM is a startup error, not a callback that fails later.
#
# Apple Developer → Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles:
#   Identifiers → Services IDs → new → enable Sign in with Apple → Configure:
#     Domain:        <the host of EAIT__BACKEND__PUBLIC_API_URL>
#     Return URL:    ${EAIT__BACKEND__PUBLIC_API_URL}/start/auth/apple/callback   (https, never localhost)
#   Keys → new → Sign in with Apple → download the .p8 ONCE; the Key ID is on that page.
#   The Team ID is top right of the portal.
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_SERVICE_ID=
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_TEAM_ID=
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_KEY_ID=
EAIT__BACKEND__APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Where a finished web onboarding sends somebody to subscribe — a RevenueCat Web Billing paywall
# link, or anything else that ends in a delivery to /v1/revenuecat/webhook. MUST CONTAIN {userId},
# and the server refuses to boot without it: the webhook is the only thing that can grant an
# entitlement, app_user_id is how it names the account, and a checkout that does not carry one
# produces a purchase the server refuses and never retries. Empty = the plan page offers nothing.
EAIT__BACKEND__WEB_CHECKOUT_URL=

# The app-side halves live in `src/mobile/.env.example`, NOT here — Expo reads env files from the
# mobile project directory, so putting them in this file would set them for the server and for
# nothing else. They must match EAIT__BACKEND__GOOGLE_AUDIENCES above, or every Google sign-in fails audience
# verification. That file also carries EXPO_PUBLIC_EAIT__FRONTEND__API_URL, which has no default anywhere.

# ── Sessions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How many days a bearer token survives WITHOUT BEING USED. Idle time, not absolute age: a phone
# that opens the app once a month never signs out, and one that was sold six months ago stops
# working. The stored form is a SHA-256 hash, so a database dump names accounts and login times
# without letting the reader become any of them (src/platform/src/backend/auth/tokens.ts).
#
# Must be at least 1. Zero would expire every token the instant it was issued, which presents as an
# app that cannot stay signed in and as nothing at all in any log.
EAIT__BACKEND__SESSION_TTL_DAYS=180

# ── Per-address limits ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOT the spend caps above. Those bound one account and the whole instance, and both were
# bypassable in one line of curl: POST /v1/auth/device mints an account for anybody, so resetting a
# per-user allowance cost one request. These bound the ADDRESS instead, which is what makes the
# per-account number mean anything.
#
# Held in memory, so a restart forgives everyone and a second replica would double every limit.
# Fine for one container behind one proxy; if that changes, so must src/platform/src/backend/api/ratelimit.ts.
#
# CARRIER-GRADE NAT IS THE COST. A mobile network can put thousands of subscribers behind one
# address, so these are set generously on purpose — refusing a stranger who logged one meal is a
# worse failure than the spend being guarded. Zero disables a limit, explicitly.
EAIT__BACKEND__AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=20
EAIT__BACKEND__ANALYSIS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_DAY=60
# The thread's own lines (POST /v1/messages/lines) and the manual edit (PATCH /v1/meals/:id): unbilled, bounded
# per address like the health sync — one allowance, two counters.
EAIT__BACKEND__LINES_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=120
EAIT__BACKEND__SUBSCRIBE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=5
# The health sync. Nothing is billed on this route, which is exactly why it needs its own bound:
# it is the heaviest write the API accepts (up to MAX_HEALTH_DAYS_PER_BATCH upserts per request in
# one transaction) and no spend cap covers it. The app syncs once per visit to one screen.
EAIT__BACKEND__HEALTH_SYNC_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=120

# ── The mailing list, and the one email this product sends ────────────────────────────────────
# A submission is NOT a subscription: the address is stored pending, one confirmation goes out, and
# only the link in it adds anybody. Unconfirmed rows are deleted after EAIT__BACKEND__SUBSCRIBE_CONFIRM_TTL_DAYS —
# an address somebody typed into a form and never confirmed is personal data held with no basis for
# it, quite possibly somebody else's.
#
# EAIT__BACKEND__MAIL_PROVIDER=log prints the confirmation link (and never the recipient) instead of sending, which
# is what makes the whole flow drivable in development with no vendor account. In production that
# means nobody can ever confirm, and the server says so loudly at startup.
#
# EAIT__BACKEND__MAIL_FROM must be on a domain verified with the provider or every send is a 403.
EAIT__BACKEND__MAIL_PROVIDER=log
EAIT__BACKEND__MAIL_FROM=eait <lets@eait.fit>
EAIT__BACKEND__RESEND_API_KEY=
EAIT__BACKEND__RESEND_BASE_URL=https://api.resend.com
EAIT__BACKEND__MAIL_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
EAIT__BACKEND__SUBSCRIBE_CONFIRM_TTL_DAYS=7

# This server's own public origin, used to build the confirmation link. Empty works it out from the
# request — the Host Caddy forwards plus X-Forwarded-Proto — which is fine in development. Set it
# wherever it is known: a link built from a header is a link whose hostname a client can influence,
# and this one goes into an email.
EAIT__BACKEND__PUBLIC_API_URL=

# Where the BROWSER is, when that is not the same host (#406). The OAuth redirect_uri is built from
# this — it is where a provider returns the person — while the confirmation link above is a path on
# the API and stays with it. Empty means "the same origin as the API", which is what development
# wants and what every host did before the split.
EAIT__BACKEND__PUBLIC_WEB_URL=

# The one account made an admin at boot, by user id (#391a). A UUID, never a provider `sub`: the
# identities table is keyed (provider, subject) and a device identity's subject is any string the
# client chose, so a grant keyed on a subject would be claimable by anyone willing to mint one.
# Applied idempotently on every start, because a restored dump must not leave an instance nobody
# can administer. Empty means nobody is an admin, and the admin surface answers 404.
EAIT__BACKEND__ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_USER_ID=

# ── The admin ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Onboarding copy and the onboarding funnel, at /admin.
#
# UNSET MEANS THERE IS NO ADMIN: every path under /admin answers 404, so a deployment that never
# sets this has no admin surface to attack rather than a locked one to guess at. Set-and-short is a
# STARTUP ERROR (minimum 24 characters) — it would look protected and not be.
#
# This is a real secret. It edits what every new user reads while answering questions about their
# health, and it is its own authority: a user's bearer token gets nothing here, and this gets
# nothing on the user API. Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# EAIT__BACKEND__ADMIN_TOKEN is RETIRED (#391b). The admin is a role an account carries; grant it
# with EAIT__BACKEND__ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_USER_ID above.

# ── The paid tier (RevenueCat) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The App Store takes the payment, RevenueCat is told, and RevenueCat tells this server here. There
# is no other way an account becomes paid — no client route grants an entitlement, and there must
# never be one.
#
# UNSET MEANS THERE IS NO WEBHOOK: /v1/revenuecat/webhook answers 404, nobody is ever entitled, and
# every account gets the free cap. That is the correct default for development and for any host
# that is not selling anything. Set-and-short is a STARTUP ERROR (minimum 24 characters, no
# whitespace) — what is behind it is the ability to write "has paid" onto any account whose id you
# can name, and account ids are handed to clients.
#
# The value is compared against the WHOLE Authorization header, byte for byte and in constant time.
# Paste exactly this string into RevenueCat's dashboard (Integrations → Webhooks → Authorization
# header) — if you type "Bearer xyz" there, this variable is "Bearer xyz", spaces and all, which
# this server refuses. Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -d '\n=+/'
EAIT__BACKEND__REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=

# WHICH RevenueCat entitlement grants the paid tier. A project can carry several — a lifetime
# unlock, a legacy plan, an internal comp — and an event names the ones it affects. Matching a
# configured id rather than "any entitlement at all" is what stops a product nobody meant to sell
# the tier from selling it. Must match the entitlement identifier in the RevenueCat dashboard.
EAIT__BACKEND__REVENUECAT_ENTITLEMENT_ID=pro

# ── Notifications ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three messages and no others: the two trial reminders, which the PHONE fires locally off
# entitlement.expiresAt, and the 20:30 line, which this server composes from the day's meals and
# pushes through Expo. One a day including the reminders — a reminder day sends the reminder
# INSTEAD OF the evening line.
#
# OFF unless this is set. It is also the switch to pull if the loop ever does more harm than good:
# the retention plan pre-commits to turning a loop off when its blocks exceed its reactivations,
# and that promise needs a switch rather than a code change.
EAIT__BACKEND__PUSH_ENABLED=false

# Expo's push access token. A real secret, and redact() masks it.
#
# UNSET MEANS PUSHES ARE LOGGED, NOT SENT — even with PUSH_ENABLED on. Expo accepts unauthenticated
# sends, so anybody who learns a device token could push to it; this server will not make such a
# send, and falls back to logging with one warning at boot. Get one from
# expo.dev → your account → Settings → Access tokens, and turn on "Enhanced Security for Push
# Notifications" in the project's settings.
EAIT__BACKEND__EXPO_PUSH_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# The Telegram connector (src/backend/telegram/): a bot token from @BotFather. A SECRET. EMPTY MEANS NO
# CONNECTOR — no polling, no Connect Telegram link, one boot line saying it is off — and --demo never
# reads it. Use a different bot in development than in production: one token polled by two
# processes answers 409 to both. A wrong token stops the connector with a log line, never the API.
EAIT__BACKEND__TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# When the evening line goes out, HH:MM, in EAIT__BACKEND__TZ_NAME. Malformed is a STARTUP ERROR,
# not a fallback: a typo here produces a server that sends at a time nobody chose, once a day, and
# the only way to find out is to notice when the messages arrive. 20:30 is what the onboarding copy
# promises out loud (product/design/onboarding/copy.md § Step 18) — moving it makes that false.
EAIT__BACKEND__EVENING_LINE_TIME=20:30

# How long one push request may hang before it is abandoned.
EAIT__BACKEND__PUSH_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# IANA zone for every date boundary. NOT UTC — a daily total whose midnight is UTC rolls over at
# 01:00 local, so a late dinner lands on tomorrow.
EAIT__BACKEND__TZ_NAME=Europe/Berlin

# ── Developer tooling, never read by the server ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `bun scripts/asc.ts` reads App Store Connect — which products exist, which devices are
# registered — so a release checklist can be CHECKED rather than looked at. Read-only: every
# subcommand is a GET.
#
# The issuer id is at App Store Connect → Users and Access → Integrations, above the key list, and
# is shared between the API keys and the In-App Purchase keys. If none is shown there, generate any
# App Store Connect API key and it appears. RevenueCat wants the same value.
#
# NOT namespaced `EAIT__BACKEND__*` on purpose: this is tooling input, like the EAIT_* worktree
# values and TEST_DATABASE_URL, and nothing in the running product reads it.
# EAIT__TOOLING__ASC_KEY_ID=9VC5UXHK4B
# EAIT__TOOLING__ASC_ISSUER_ID=
# The .p8 itself. Defaults to ~/.config/eait/AuthKey_<key id>.p8, falling back to ~/.config/ieat/
# from before the rename — that directory is outside this repo, so the rename could not move it.
# EAIT__TOOLING__ASC_KEY_PATH=
